# The `db` object and how it gets wired up

## Module-scope, app-less, at first

`src/{{ package_name }}/core/db.py` starts with:

```python
from flask import Flask
from flask_sqlalchemy import SQLAlchemy
{% if migrations %}from flask_migrate import Migrate
{% endif %}

db = SQLAlchemy()
{% if migrations %}migrate = Migrate()
{% endif %}
```

`db = SQLAlchemy()` at import time does **no I/O** — it just builds an
unconfigured extension instance. This is what makes it safe to import
`db` from anywhere (`models.py` does, to subclass `db.Model`) without
accidentally requiring a live database connection just to import a
module. It's the same reason `create_app()` is a factory function
instead of a module-level `app = Flask(__name__)`: see the `flask`
skill for that half of the pattern.

## `init_db(app, *, testing=False)` does the real binding

```python
def init_db(app: Flask, *, testing: bool = False) -> None:
    database_url = app.config["SQLALCHEMY_DATABASE_URI"]
    if database_url.startswith("sqlite"):
        engine_options = {"connect_args": {"check_same_thread": False}}
        if ":memory:" in database_url:
            from sqlalchemy.pool import StaticPool
            engine_options["poolclass"] = StaticPool
        app.config["SQLALCHEMY_ENGINE_OPTIONS"] = engine_options

    db.init_app(app)
{% if migrations %}    migrate.init_app(app, db)
{% endif %}    ...
```

Called from `create_app()` in `main.py`, after
`app.config["SQLALCHEMY_DATABASE_URI"]` has been set from `Settings`
(or from a test-supplied `database_url` — see `tests/conftest.py`).
`db.init_app(app)` is the step that actually reads that config key and
attaches engine/session machinery to *this* `Flask` instance — before
this call, `db` exists but isn't pointed at any database.

Two things worth knowing about the SQLite branch:

- `SQLALCHEMY_ENGINE_OPTIONS` is set on `app.config` *before*
  `db.init_app(app)` runs, because that's when Flask-SQLAlchemy reads
  it to build the engine — setting it after `init_app` has no effect.
- `StaticPool` only kicks in for `sqlite:///:memory:` specifically (the
  test suite's database, see `tests/conftest.py`). A `:memory:`
  database only exists for the lifetime of a single connection; the
  default pool hands out a fresh connection per checkout, which would
  silently lose every table and row between requests. A real
  file-based SQLite path or a Postgres URL doesn't need this.

{% if migrations %}
## `migrate.init_app(app, db)` needs the bound `db`

Because this project has `migrations=true`, `core/db.py` also creates
`migrate = Migrate()` and calls `migrate.init_app(app, db)` right after
`db.init_app(app)`. `Migrate` needs a reference to the *same* `db`
object your models are defined against so Alembic's autogenerate can
diff your `db.Model` subclasses against the live database schema.
`migrate.init_app` is also what registers the `flask db ...` CLI
command group in the first place — without it, `flask db migrate`/
`flask db upgrade` don't exist as commands at all.

The actual migration workflow (`flask db migrate -m "..."`,
`flask db upgrade`, editing a generated migration file) is the
`flask-migrate` skill's territory, not this one — this skill only
covers the object wiring that makes those commands available.
{% endif %}

## `db.Model` vs. a bare `DeclarativeBase`

`models.py` subclasses `db.Model`, not a plain SQLAlchemy
`DeclarativeBase`:

```python
class Item(db.Model):
    id: Mapped[int] = mapped_column(primary_key=True)
    name: Mapped[str]
    description: Mapped[str | None] = mapped_column(default=None)
```

`db.Model` is Flask-SQLAlchemy's declarative base, pre-wired to the
same `db` instance that `init_db()` binds to the app — that's what
lets `db.session`, `db.select(...)`, and table metadata
(`db.metadata`, used by `db.create_all()`) all agree on the same set
of models without you having to pass anything explicitly. Defining a
model against a *different* `SQLAlchemy()` instance than the one
`init_app`-bound to your app is a common way to get "table not found"
errors that look like a migrations problem but aren't — always import
`db` from `core/db.py` for every model.

Column syntax (`Mapped[...]` + `mapped_column(...)`, relationships via
`db.relationship(...)`) is plain SQLAlchemy 2.0 declarative syntax —
see the `sqlalchemy` skill for the general patterns; nothing about it
changes because the base class happens to be `db.Model`.
